Here is an example of a C++ program that uses templates to evaluate an Nth Fibonacci number:
```
#include <iostream>

template <int N>
using fib = int;

template <int N>
constexpr fib<N> fibonacci() {
  if (N == 0) return 0;
  if (N == 1) return 1;
  return fib<N-1>() + fib<N-2>();
}

int main() {
  std::cout << fibonacci<10>() << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```
This program defines a template called `fib` that represents an Nth Fibonacci number, and a function called `fibonacci` that uses recursion to calculate the Nth Fibonacci number. The `main` function then calls the `fibonacci` function with an argument of 10, and the result is printed to the console.

Note that this program uses constant expression (i.e. `constexpr`) to define the `fibonacci` function, which allows the function to be evaluated at compile time, rather than at runtime. This makes the program more efficient, since it avoids the overhead of calling a function repeatedly at runtime.
